{"author":{"id":"86dc3c885e4fa466d19699d4a5e74c240f1c9d6b23f4e62ea2d2d8e359bd1711"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1790713548,"nonce":"Z9/eF4LIZEkwHLVHOXY/h27dzNo=","target":"02e51b7a5e2dacf47fd278481561e3808fda3e68ce7c27c846d8ad8e76820909","message":"\u003c!-- ABOUTME: Defines sketch description authoring, desktop sketch bundles, and the Sketches gallery. --\u003e\n\u003c!-- ABOUTME: Records agreed scope, persistence behavior, and acceptance testing for implementation planning. --\u003e\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nStudents can save and reopen GIC source, but cannot yet maintain an in-app gallery of their own sketches with descriptions and previews. Bundled Examples already demonstrate the desired presentation and metadata format. Students need the same ability for their own work without manually authoring YAML or generating preview images.\n\nDesktop sketch saves already use one folder per sketch in the configured projects folder's sketchbook. Those folders provide a natural home for descriptions and thumbnails. The browser/PWA has a different capability boundary: opening a file reads it into memory, and saving downloads a source file without retaining a writable link to the original. It cannot offer the same native folder discovery and sidecar persistence in this feature.\n\n## Solution\n\nAdd a Description panel for the active sketch in the shared IDE. Students edit the Markdown description separately from fields labelled exactly `title`, `order`, `enabled`, `categories`, and `tags`.\n\nOn explicit desktop Save or Save As, save the source and write `description.md` when the Markdown body has content. Generate its YAML frontmatter using the same schema as bundled example descriptions. If a previously populated description is cleared, delete that sketch's `description.md` on the next save; keep the source and thumbnail.\n\nOn saves with a current successful preview, write `thumbnail.png` from the displayed Canvas at native resolution, without rerunning the sketch. Broken code remains saveable. A failed preview must not replace a previously saved thumbnail, and a sketch without a thumbnail uses a gallery placeholder.\n\nProvide a desktop gallery labelled exactly **Sketches**, distinct from immutable **Examples**. Load only the sketchbook in the currently configured projects folder. A sketch is visible only when it has a valid description and `enabled: true`; sketches without descriptions and sketches with `enabled: false` are hidden. Sort cards by `order`, then title. Selecting a card opens the saved sketch for continued editing.\n\nThe browser/PWA includes the Description panel but retains its source-file open/download workflow. It does not persist description or thumbnail sidecars or discover a native sketch gallery.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As a student, I want a Description panel for my active sketch, so that I can explain its intent alongside the code.\n2. As a student, I want separate metadata fields and a Markdown body editor, so that I do not need to learn YAML to describe my work.\n3. As a student, I want the metadata labels to be `title`, `order`, `enabled`, `categories`, and `tags`, so that the panel matches the saved frontmatter.\n4. As a student, I want to edit the sketch title, so that the gallery can present a meaningful name for my work.\n5. As a student, I want to edit categories and tags, so that my sketches carry the same descriptive metadata as bundled examples.\n6. As a student, I want an editable `order` field defaulting to `0`, so that I can arrange my gallery without changing filenames.\n7. As a student, I want new descriptions to default to `enabled: true`, so that saving a populated description makes the sketch eligible for my gallery.\n8. As a student, I want an `enabled` checkbox, so that I can hide a sketch without deleting its description or source.\n9. As a desktop student, I want Save to write my populated description and frontmatter beside the source, so that the sketch stays portable and inspectable through ordinary filesystem tools.\n10. As a desktop student, I want Save As to carry my current description into the saved sketch copy, so that copying a sketch does not lose its explanation.\n11. As a desktop student, I want to reopen a sketch with its saved description and metadata, so that I can continue editing the complete sketch rather than only its code.\n12. As a desktop student, I want clearing the Markdown body and saving to remove `description.md`, so that the saved bundle and gallery reflect my decision to remove the description.\n13. As a student, I want description and metadata edits to participate in the active document's unsaved-change protection, so that switching sketches does not silently discard them.\n14. As a desktop student, I want a thumbnail captured from the current successful Canvas preview on save, so that the gallery shows the image I was viewing.\n15. As a desktop student, I want thumbnail capture to preserve the native Canvas resolution without rerunning the sketch, so that random sketches do not change merely because I saved.\n16. As a desktop student, I want to save unfinished or broken source, so that a language error does not prevent me from preserving my work.\n17. As a desktop student, I want a failed preview to leave the saved thumbnail intact, so that saving broken code does not replace a useful image with an empty or invalid preview.\n18. As a desktop student, I want a placeholder for an otherwise visible sketch that has no thumbnail, so that lack of a successful preview does not prevent gallery access.\n19. As a desktop student, I want a gallery named Sketches, so that my own saved work is clearly distinguished from Examples.\n20. As a desktop student, I want sketches without descriptions or with `enabled: false` excluded from Sketches, so that gallery participation is deliberate.\n21. As a desktop student, I want cards sorted by `order` and then title, so that the gallery arrangement is predictable.\n22. As a desktop student, I want selecting a gallery card to open that saved sketch, so that I can resume work through the normal one-document workflow.\n23. As a desktop student, I want successful saves to update the gallery's content and visibility, so that it reflects my saved descriptions, metadata, and thumbnails.\n24. As a desktop student, I want changing the configured projects folder to change the gallery's discovery scope, so that I see only the current project's saved work.\n25. As a desktop student, I want saved descriptions and gallery entries to survive application restart, so that the gallery is built from my files rather than transient UI state.\n26. As a browser student, I want the same description authoring panel without misleading storage promises, so that I understand that source downloads do not update a native sketch bundle.\n27. As a student, I want bundled Examples to remain immutable, so that personal sketch saves and gallery changes do not modify shipped teaching material.\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n### Shared authoring and document lifecycle\n\n- Keep the existing shared React/Monaco/Canvas IDE and one-active-document workflow. Do not introduce a second editor application or multiple-document model.\n- Add Description as workspace panel content using the established FlexLayout composition and persisted-layout conventions. Moving or switching panels must retain the active description edits.\n- Represent the Markdown body and typed metadata separately in active sketch state; serialize them into a description document at the desktop save boundary.\n- Use the existing example metadata schema: a non-empty string `title`, finite numeric `order`, boolean `enabled`, and string-list `categories` and `tags`.\n- Make all five fields editable under their exact frontmatter names. Use a checkbox labelled `enabled`, initially checked, and default `order` to `0`.\n- A blank or whitespace-only Markdown body counts as no description. Metadata alone does not create `description.md`.\n- Opening a saved desktop sketch loads its source and description together. Opening a sketch without a description starts with empty description content; do not carry another sketch's description forward.\n- New sketch, Open, gallery selection, and example selection must respect description-only unsaved changes as well as source changes. Reuse the existing dirty indication, discard confirmation, and private recovery lifecycle rather than adding a competing save mechanism.\n- Keep explicit source saving, private recovery, and immutable example copying distinct. No background write of source or description files is introduced.\n- Preserve existing format-on-save semantics: formatting happens once within explicit saving and does not turn thumbnail capture into another language execution.\n- The browser Description panel is in-memory authoring, not native sidecar persistence. Source download must not reset or silently discard the current description merely because source was downloaded, and the UI must not claim that the description was saved to a linked file.\n\n### Desktop sketch bundle persistence\n\n- Extend the typed desktop document bridge and native document storage workflow to carry description data and optional captured PNG bytes alongside source.\n- Use the existing one-folder-per-sketch structure. Write only the active sketch's source, `description.md`, and `thumbnail.png`; preserve sessions and unrelated user files.\n- Generate example-compatible YAML frontmatter. Correctly round-trip punctuation, Unicode, multiline Markdown, and category/tag string lists rather than constructing ambiguous YAML by concatenating unchecked input.\n- Save and Save As use the same description and thumbnail rules. Save As preserves the original sketch and saves the current description into the copy; retain existing tutor-session copy behavior.\n- On the next explicit save after the Markdown body is cleared, delete an existing `description.md` in that sketch folder. Do not delete the source, thumbnail, sessions, or folder.\n- Broken GIC source is not a save validation failure. Save source and populated description even when parsing, analysis, execution, or preview timeout has failed.\n- Preserve existing safe file-replacement practices and surface native I/O failures. Cancellation is a normal outcome; failed or cancelled saves must not falsely mark changes as successfully persisted.\n- Do not change the existing ability to open a source file through the native file dialog, or automatically relocate standalone source files. Gallery discovery remains confined to the configured sketchbook.\n\n### Thumbnail capture\n\n- Capture the currently successful Canvas preview at native pixel dimensions, without resizing, cropping, or an additional execution of GIC.\n- Reuse existing preview-freshness and PNG-generation behavior. Never capture a cleared failed preview, a superseded worker result, or another active sketch's Canvas as a successful thumbnail.\n- A failed or otherwise unavailable successful preview does not block source or description saving. When no replacement image is available, leave an existing saved thumbnail unchanged.\n- A visible sketch with no saved thumbnail uses a placeholder. A thumbnail is not a requirement for gallery eligibility.\n- Saving is not a promise of reproducibility: randomness may produce a different preview after reopening. The thumbnail records the accepted preview at save time.\n\n### Sketches discovery and presentation\n\n- Label the personal gallery exactly Sketches. Keep it distinct from Examples and make native discovery desktop-only.\n- Discover sketch bundles only within the sketchbook of the currently configured projects folder. Do not scan previous project locations, arbitrary opened-file directories, or other folders.\n- A gallery entry requires an associated saved source and a valid `description.md` whose `enabled` value is `true`. Source validity as a GIC program is not an eligibility requirement; broken code can remain visible and openable.\n- Omit sketches with no description and sketches with `enabled: false`. A missing thumbnail does not exclude an otherwise eligible sketch.\n- Present saved title, description, and thumbnail or placeholder in cards using the existing example-gallery presentation conventions. Sort by numeric `order`, then title.\n- Selecting a card opens the real saved source and its description through the native one-document lifecycle, with normal discard protection. It does not create an immutable example copy.\n- Refresh the catalog after successful saves and when the configured projects folder changes; rebuild it from saved files on launch. Unsaved panel edits do not change saved gallery visibility.\n- Keep malformed or unreadable bundles from breaking discovery of unrelated valid sketches. Do not repair, overwrite, or delete user files merely because discovery cannot read their metadata.\n\n### Architecture and trust boundaries\n\n- Keep GIC's language core browser-neutral and unchanged by gallery storage. Source execution remains interpreted through the shared core; Canvas rendering remains on the shared UI side.\n- Native filesystem access remains behind operation-specific Rust commands and approved document/gallery identities. Do not expose an unrestricted filesystem API to the webview.\n- Reuse the host-neutral content schema and appropriate shared parsing/validation behavior; runtime folder discovery is a native host responsibility, not Vite build-time content discovery.\n- Treat user descriptions as user-authored content, not automatically as trusted bundled HTML. Markdown presentation must not grant arbitrary script execution or unrestricted file access.\n- Preserve the managed-support-file ownership rules: user sketch bundles are not managed assets, and repair/update/uninstall must not reconcile or delete them.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\nFabian confirmed the following testing approach before publication. Tests should establish externally observable behavior, not implementation details or mocked file operations.\n\n1. **Primary acceptance: the packaged desktop workflow.** Exercise the real shared editor, native bridge, Canvas, and files in a real projects folder. Create a sketch, edit every description field, save, inspect the resulting bundle, find it in Sketches, reopen it, and verify source and description after application restart. Extend this workflow with Save As, `enabled: false`, clearing the description, broken-source saving, thumbnail preservation, no-thumbnail placeholders, and changing the configured projects folder. Include a packaged macOS check; browser Playwright alone is not evidence that native dialogs or the packaged webview work.\n2. **Automated native integration: the existing document-storage seam with real temporary files.** Test complete save/discover/reopen behavior rather than isolated mocked calls. Verify typed frontmatter round-trips, exact saved source, description deletion, disabled/no-description exclusion, order/title sorting, missing-thumbnail eligibility, failed-preview thumbnail preservation, Save As behavior, project-folder scoping, unreadable metadata isolation, and preservation of unrelated files. Existing native document round-trip, sketch-folder creation, opaque-ID, and real-file persistence tests provide the prior art.\n3. **Automated shared UI: the existing Playwright browser seam.** Drive the real editor, Description controls, worker, and Canvas. Cover exact field labels and defaults, description-only dirty/discard behavior, document switching without metadata leakage, and the unchanged source-file open/download workflow without a native gallery or sidecar-storage claim. Existing document-workflow, workspace-layout, example-content, and PNG-export tests provide the prior art.\n4. **Thumbnail evidence.** Inspect real generated PNG dimensions and pixels against the accepted Canvas preview. Include a random sketch to establish that save captures the existing preview rather than rerunning it, and a failed preview to establish that the prior saved thumbnail is not replaced. Preserve the existing current-source and stale-worker protections.\n5. **Metadata compatibility.** Round-trip saved descriptions through the actual example-compatible schema/validation behavior. Assert values and rendered/user-visible results, not generated YAML key order or private helper structure.\n6. **Save failure and cancellation.** Check that the original bundle remains usable, unrelated files are untouched, and the UI does not report a cancelled or failed write as a completed save. Capture and validate expected error output instead of leaving noisy test logs.\n7. **No mock substitutes for acceptance.** Do not add a test-only desktop bridge, mock filesystem, or hidden DOM state to simulate the gallery workflow. If automation cannot drive the packaged native UI, document and perform the packaged acceptance check rather than claiming browser tests cover it.\n8. **Quality gates.** Run the established shared UI and desktop tests, type checks, lint/format checks, browser build, native formatting/Clippy, and packaged desktop build for the affected implementation. Distinguish a successful package build from an actually exercised packaged workflow. Follow the project's macOS-first validation preference; retain cross-platform release checks in the existing desktop release work.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- A browser/PWA personal gallery, native folder scanning, persistent File System Access handles, origin-private sketch storage, or persistent description/thumbnail sidecars in the browser.\n- Browser folder import/export, archive-based sketch bundles, or a new portable file format.\n- Discovering sketches outside the current configured projects folder, indexing multiple project locations, or migrating old sketches when that setting changes.\n- Gallery search, category/tag filtering, additional sorting controls, drag-to-reorder, or folder management. The agreed editable `order` field and order/title sort remain in scope.\n- Cloud synchronization, accounts, sharing, publishing, or collaboration.\n- Gallery-driven renaming, moving, or deleting entire sketches. Removing `description.md` after the body is cleared is explicitly in scope.\n- Multiple-document editing, general project exploration, or arbitrary native filesystem access.\n- Expanding the GIC language, animation, image loading, or new rendering backends.\n- Rerunning source to manufacture a thumbnail, thumbnail resizing/cropping controls, or reproducibility guarantees for random previews.\n- Changing bundled Examples, rewriting their descriptions, or including user sketches in bundled tutor/example search.\n- Replacing existing desktop packaging, managed-support-file, credential, or tutor-session architecture.\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- Parent product requirements: git-bug issue `60b2077`, **PRD: GIC v0.9 workshop environment**.\n- Existing related work: git-bug issue `948a98c`, **description**, and git-bug issue `0ac47e5`, **Browse and reopen saved sketches**. This PRD records the authoring, thumbnail, gallery-visibility, project-scope, and browser-boundary decisions needed by that work; it does not silently close or duplicate those implementation issues.\n- The conversation initially considered displaying every saved sketch. Fabian's final decision supersedes that: sketches without descriptions are not visible, and `enabled: false` hides a sketch.\n- A populated description with `enabled: false` remains saved on disk. Hiding is different from clearing the body and removing the description file.\n- An existing thumbnail retained after a failed preview may represent an earlier successful version. This is deliberate; it must not be presented as proof that the currently saved source executes successfully.\n- Accepted desktop-shell/filesystem, managed-files, and workspace-layout decisions continue to govern implementation. In particular, changing the projects folder re-points the workspace rather than migrating existing sketches.\n- Publish this PRD through git-bug and its configured synchronization bridge. The tracker has no `ready-for-agent` triage label at publication time; do not invent one or apply unrelated milestone labels.\n- Implementation slicing follows PRD publication. Prefer demoable vertical slices over separate layers, and reuse the existing description and saved-sketch issues where their scope matches rather than creating competing tasks.","files":null}]}